@namespace Estimation.ArtPlanning.Components

<MudDialog>
    <DialogContent>
        <div style="max-height:70vh; overflow-y:auto" class="pr-2">

            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
                Every resource on the ART contributes their whole pool of available days to a
                <b>single skill</b> &mdash; the one they are most useful for &mdash; and the table adds those pools up
                per skill. Nobody is split across several skills.
            </MudText>

            <MudPaper Elevation="0" Class="pa-2 mb-3 rounded"
                      Style="background: var(--mud-palette-background-gray); font-family: monospace">
                capacity(skill) = Σ available_days × level_ratio × role_coefficient × train_coefficient
            </MudPaper>

            <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

            <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">1. The days that count</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
                The window runs from the PI's start date to <b>three weeks before its end date</b>. That last tail is
                the innovation and planning sprint, which is not delivery capacity. It is a flat calendar rule: the
                teams' own sprint records are not consulted, so a PI shorter than three weeks produces no capacity
                at all.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
                Within the window, each person starts with every <b>Monday to Friday</b>. Then:
            </MudText>
            <ul class="mb-3">
                <li>
                    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
                        <b>Public holidays</b> remove the day outright: those of the person's <b>city</b> and those of
                        the city's <b>country</b>, a date on both lists counting once. Somebody with no city set loses
                        nothing.
                    </MudText>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
                        <b>Personal leave</b> costs the whole day when the whole day is taken, and half a day for a
                        morning or afternoon half day.
                    </MudText>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
                        Somebody on <b>several of the ART's teams</b> is counted once: their days are divided by the
                        number of teams they sit on.
                    </MudText>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

            <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">2. Choosing each person's one skill</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
                Everything here is driven by the <b>Settings</b> page. A person's candidate skills are the skills they
                hold, narrowed and ordered like this:
            </MudText>
            <ol class="mb-3">
                <li>
                    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
                        <b>Role skill limits.</b> If their team role is listed there, only the skills allowed for
                        that role are candidates. If they hold none of them they contribute nothing at all. Somebody
                        who holds a limited role on one team and an ordinary role on another is treated as the
                        limited role.
                    </MudText>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
                        <b>Skill levels.</b> Only levels that have a row on the Skill levels tab can be chosen. A
                        level with no row is dropped before ranking rather than scored zero, so somebody whose skills
                        are all at an unlisted level drops out entirely.
                    </MudText>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
                        <b>Order of usage</b>, then <b>skill priority</b>. The lowest order of usage wins; ties fall
                        through to the lowest skill ranking.
                    </MudText>
                </li>
            </ol>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
                The winning skill takes the person's entire day pool. Each person contributes to exactly one
                skill, however many of the ART's teams they sit on.
            </MudText>

            <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

            <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">3. Turning days into capacity</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
                Three multipliers are applied to the available days, all from Settings:
            </MudText>
            <ul class="mb-3">
                <li>
                    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
                        <b>Level ratio</b> &mdash; how much of a working day that skill level delivers. A ratio of 0 is
                        allowed and means the level contributes nothing while still occupying the person's one skill.
                    </MudText>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
                        <b>Role coefficient</b> &mdash; scales a whole team role, for roles that spend part of their
                        time away from delivery. A role that is not listed counts as 1.
                    </MudText>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
                        <b>Train coefficient</b> &mdash; one multiplier for the whole ART, applied last to each skill
                        total. A value of 1 leaves the numbers unchanged.
                    </MudText>
                </li>
            </ul>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
                Open the <b>Members</b> count on any row to see each person's days and every multiplier that was
                applied to them.
            </MudText>

            <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

            <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">4. The Unassigned row</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
                Unassigned is not an error row: it is where working days go when no skill could be chosen. Its
                capacity is always zero, but its working days are real. Somebody lands there when they have:
            </MudText>
            <ul class="mb-3">
                <li><MudText Typo="Typo.body2">no skill at a level listed on the Skill levels tab;</MudText></li>
                <li><MudText Typo="Typo.body2">no team role;</MudText></li>
                <li><MudText Typo="Typo.body2">no employee number, which is how people are matched to their skills;</MudText></li>
                <li><MudText Typo="Typo.body2">a limited role but none of the skills that role allows.</MudText></li>
            </ul>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
                <b>Inactive members and teams with no members</b> also land here, as nameless rows that still accrue
                working days. That is why the total can look larger than the people you expect. The chips above the
                table count them.
            </MudText>

            <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

            <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">5. Rules that surprise people</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
                These are all agreed behaviour rather than defects. They are listed because each one can make a number
                look wrong at a glance.
            </MudText>
            <MudSimpleTable Dense Bordered Striped Class="mb-3">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th style="width:34%">Rule</th>
                        <th>What it means</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Unranked skills</td>
                        <td>
                            A skill with no ranking sorts <b>ahead</b> of every ranked one, so leaving a skill blank
                            makes it win ties rather than lose them.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Roles across ARTs</td>
                        <td>
                            Skill selection looks at every ART. Somebody on teams in two ARTs may have the role they
                            hold elsewhere decide the skill and coefficient used here.
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </MudSimpleTable>

            <MudDivider Class="my-3" />

            <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="font-weight-bold mb-2">6. Where the settings come from</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
                Settings are held per ART and PI. The PI dropdown on the Settings page also offers <b>Default</b>:
                values saved there apply to any PI of that ART that has none of its own.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-2">
                The fallback is <b>per tab</b>. Saving only the skill priorities for a PI leaves its level ratios,
                coefficients and limits still following the Default. The banner above the table says when a PI is
                running on the Default.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
                Nothing is configured until somebody configures it &mdash; an ART with empty settings puts every
                resource in Unassigned. <b>Copy from previous</b> fills a PI from the most recent earlier PI that has
                settings, for when only small changes are needed.
            </MudText>

            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mud-text-secondary">
                The table is rebuilt from the current settings and resource data every time this page is opened.
            </MudText>
        </div>
    </DialogContent>
    <DialogActions>
        <MudButton OnClick="Close">Close</MudButton>
    </DialogActions>
</MudDialog>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter] private IMudDialogInstance MudDialog { get; set; } = null!;

    private void Close() => MudDialog.Close();
}
